INT. AUCTION HOUSE - FREIGHT ELEVATOR - NIGHT

A caged industrial lift with a scarred wooden floor. A control panel with a brass key switch. The elevator descends slowly, cables creaking, between concrete shaft walls.

Three figures in grey janitorial coveralls stand in the car. A rolling mop bucket sits in the corner.

HOLLIS CARMICHAEL-REEVE (50s, crew boss, bifocals on a cord) reads a clipboard like a recipe.

TRINH VO (30s, wiry, hair under a cap) crouches by the bucket and checks the wringer for something that isn't water.

ALOUETTE PICARD (40s, grim, gloves tight) presses her palm against the elevator wall and counts the seconds under her breath.

HOLLIS
Sub-basement in forty.

TRINH
Thirty-eight.

HOLLIS
Thirty-eight.

ALOUETTE
Camera's looping. Twenty minutes, maybe twenty-two.

TRINH
Maybe?

ALOUETTE
Twenty.

HOLLIS
Say twenty.

Silence. The cables squeal. The lift lurches and settles. Somewhere above, a muffled bell rings the hour.

HOLLIS (CONT'D)
When the doors open, we are cleaners. We move slow. We do not look at the vault.

TRINH
We look at the floor.

HOLLIS
We look at the floor.

ALOUETTE
What if there's a guard?

HOLLIS
There's a guard.

ALOUETTE
What if he's awake?

HOLLIS
He's awake. He's always awake. Four weeks, he's seen us.

TRINH
He likes us.

ALOUETTE
He likes your biscuits.

TRINH
(blushing)
They're my mother's.

The elevator slows, and the cables go quiet. A soft ding.

The doors slide open on a concrete corridor lit in sickly green. At the far end, a lone guard's desk with a lamp, a thermos, and a newspaper. The chair is empty.

Nobody moves.

TRINH (CONT'D)
(whispering)
That's not right.

HOLLIS
Move.

They shuffle out, bucket first. The wheels squeak on the concrete. Halfway down the corridor, the lamp on the desk clicks off. The corridor goes darker.

ALOUETTE
Hollis.

HOLLIS
Keep moving.

TRINH
Twenty minutes, he said. He doesn't go to the bathroom.

They stop. In the dark, a steel door at the corridor's end is open a hand's width. A strip of cold light falls on the floor.

Hollis lifts the clipboard. Reads it again. Lowers it.

HOLLIS
Right. New plan.

TRINH
We have a plan?

HOLLIS
We have a mop.

He lifts the mop out of the bucket. Taps the vault door open with its handle. It swings wide. Inside, rows of locked cases line the walls under blue light. And in the middle, sitting cross-legged on a crate with a flask in one hand and his cap tilted back, is the GUARD (60s, leathery, content), eating one of Trinh's mother's biscuits.

GUARD
Evening, team.

Nobody breathes.

GUARD (CONT'D)
You're early. I wasn't expecting you till two.

TRINH
(squeaking)
We had a cancellation.

GUARD
Hm.

He chews. Swallows. Gestures with the biscuit toward the cases.

GUARD (CONT'D)
Mind the lemon polish. The Tiffany lamp's sensitive.

HOLLIS
Of course.

^ALOUETTE
Of course.

TRINH
Not a problem.

They file in. The guard watches them go by, one eyebrow raised. As Alouette passes, he catches her sleeve.

GUARD (CONT'D)
(low)
Whatever you've planned, do it tonight. I retire at dawn.

ALOUETTE
(low)
Understood.

She pulls her sleeve free. Trinh begins to laugh, a high sound, and Hollis clamps a hand over his mouth. The guard offers the biscuit tin.

GUARD (CONT'D)
Anyone?

The lights in the corridor behind them go out. Then, one by one, the lights in the vault.

HOLLIS
(to the room)
Nobody panic.

GUARD
Oh, that's just me.

TRINH
(muffled, under the hand)
Mm?

GUARD
(in the dark)
The sale starts at nine. Lot forty-one's a Tiffany lamp.

A match flares. The guard lights a candle on the crate. His grin glows orange.

GUARD (CONT'D)
Shall we?

FADE OUT.
